{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And Moshe spoke to Aharon and to Elazar and to Itamar his surviving sons.</b> Now he already mentioned above, ויאמר משה אל אהרן ולאלעזר ולאיתמר בניו, and it would have been fitting for these verses to be adjacent to one another, since all of it is Moshe's speech to Aharon and to his two sons equally. But he interrupted between them with the passage of 'wine and strong drink you shall not drink,' in order to teach you proper conduct (derekh eretz): that a person is obligated to comfort mourners. For Aharon was mourning here for his two sons Nadav and Avihu, and the Holy One, blessed be He, came to comfort him, as the Sages, blessed be their memory, interpreted the verse (Genesis 18:1) וירא אליו — 'to visit the sick.' And this is why you find the speech addressed to Aharon alone, where it says וידבר ה׳ אל אהרן לאמר. And likewise the Sages, blessed be their memory, interpreted: 'Just as He is merciful, so you too be merciful; just as He is gracious, so you too be gracious; just as He comforts mourners, so you too comfort mourners; just as He visits the sick, so you too visit the sick; just as He buries the dead, so you too bury the dead.'" "<b>And likewise</b> they said in the Midrash on Kohelet (Ecclesiastes 7:2): ״טוב ללכת אל בית אבל מלכת אל בית משתה״ — better that a person go in a practice that applies to both the living and the dead than in one that applies only to the living. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: 'Learn from Me, for I set aside the joy of My sons in the Tabernacle and revealed Myself to mourn Nadav and Avihu.' But the Sages, blessed be their memory, proved from another source that He, blessed be He, comforts mourners — from what is written (Genesis 25:11): ״ויהי אחרי מות אברהם ויברך אלהים את יצחק בנו״, and their intent in this blessing was the consolation of mourners." "<b>And you shall eat it, unleavened, beside the altar.</b> And it is written, ״ואכלתם אותה במקום קדוש״, so that you not understand it to mean literally beside the altar — therefore He added and said, ״ואכלתם אותה במקום קדוש״: He permitted them to eat it anywhere in the entire courtyard, for the whole courtyard of the Tent of Meeting is called holy. And what He said, אצל המזבח, is to teach that wherever it is eaten near the altar is better and more fitting."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:36.780471366Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}